Attested somewhat late, earliest in "Rhapsody on Gaotang [Shrine]" ({{lw|zh|s:高唐賦|高唐賦}}), attributed to {{w|Song Yu}} & included in {{w|Wen Xuan|Selections of Refined Literature}}:
* {{zh-x|王雎，鸝黃，正冥，楚鳩。|the royal-coot, the yellow '''[[witwall]] / oriole''', herald-of-dusk; warbler / dove of Chu,|CL|ref='''3rd century {{BCE}}''', {{lw|zh|宋玉}} {{w|Song Yu}}, {{lang|zh|《高唐賦》}} ("Rhapsody on Gaotang [Shrine]"); translation based on {{w|Arthur Waley}}'s version.|collapsed=yes}}

===Etymology===
{{w|Guo Pu}} ({{lw|zh|郭璞}}) glosses 鸝 as meaning " yellowish black";<ref> {{lang|zh|郭璞}} (Guo Pu), quoted in {{lang|zh|《六臣註文選》}} (''Selections of Refined Literature - Annotated by Six Ministers''), "vol. 19", [https://ctext.org/library.pl?if=gb&file=79260&page=97#%E9%B8%9D%E9%BB%83%E9%BB%92%E4%B9%9F p. 97 of 153]. quote: 「郭璞曰鸝黃黒也」</ref> and also proposes that the black-naped orioles are named {{zh-l|鸝黃}} after "their yellow and sallow black colorings"; Li Shan ({{lw|zh|李善 (唐朝)|李善}}) apparently relates the birds' color {{lang|zh|鸝／鹂}} to the horses' coat-color {{zh-l|驪|pure black}} by treating each graph as the other's variant.<ref>{{lang|zh|李善}} (Li Shan), {{lang|zh|《文選注》}} (''Selections of Refined Literature - Annotated''), "vol 18 - 23", [https://ctext.org/library.pl?if=gb&file=94382&page=71 p. 71 of 356]. quote:「鸝黃…… 驪黃郭璞曰其色黧黑而黃因名之一曰鶬鶊」</ref>

