===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}}. A cereal 禾 with water 水 or kernels under it. The water may symbolize the wine made from millet or the fact that millet is sticky and water-resistant; otherwise, given that millet is a resistant plant that can grow on dry ground, the "water" component remains unexplained. See [[黎]], in which the radical appears as a contracted form.

===Etymology===
{{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} minimally reconstructs {{cog|och||*nhaʔ}}, comparing it to {{cog|bo|ནས་||barley}}. STEDT provisionally reconstructs {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*nas||{{vern|highland barley}}}}.

However, {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (2014)}} reconstruct {{cog|och||*s-tʰaʔ}} based on aspirated affricate reflexes in certain Mandarin dialects, e.g. Hefei {{m|und||tʂʰu³}}, Yangzhou {{m|und||tsʰu³}}, as well as sound gloss evidence from ''{{w|Shuowen}}''. This would make the comparison to {{cog|bo|-}} less plausible.

